{
  "image_name": "3b386c29150a04b227d8004b5265a36e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two kinds of tumour microenvironments: a “pseudo-immune-hot” state and a truly “immune-hot” state. In both, T cells are present around tumour cells displaying antigen through MHC I, but the composition and function of those T cells differ.\n\nOn the left, the pseudo-immune-hot setting appears enriched in bystander T cells rather than tumour-specific T cells. These cells carry markers such as PD-1, CD39, IL-7R, and TCR, but the overall message is that they are not effectively recognizing tumour antigen. The central arrow summarizes this with fewer tumour-specific T cells and more bystander T cells.\n\nOn the right, the immune-hot environment contains more tumour-specific T cells actively engaging tumour cells through TCR–MHC I interactions. These T cells show activation or tissue-resident/exhaustion-associated markers including CD69, ITGAE, ZNF683, TIM3, PD-1, CD39, and LAG3. The tumour surface also shows inhibitory ligand-receptor pathways such as PD-L1, FGL1, galectin-9, and adenosine-related signaling, indicating that even active antitumour T cells are under immune suppression.\n\nOverall, the figure highlights that simple T-cell infiltration is not enough; the key distinction is whether infiltrating T cells are tumour-specific or mostly bystanders.",
